"narrative": "I have appraised the discovery results for the predictive marker hypotheses. \n\nFirst, regarding Pembrolizumab, none of the tested interactions reached clinical significance. The effect of PD-L1 >= 50% (H30) showed a small positive estimate (0.0168), but the confidence interval crossed zero and the magnitude (~0.8% relative difference) is well below the 10% threshold. Similarly, PD-L1 >= 1% (H31), adenocarcinoma histology (H32), and the high-risk markers (brain mets H33, high CRP H34, and Stage IV H35) all showed negligible effect sizes with confidence intervals crossing zero. This suggests that Pembrolizumab's efficacy is not significantly modulated by these markers in this population.\n\nSecond, I investigated whether high NLR modulates the efficacy of targeted therapies. The interactions for Sotorasib (H36), Osimertinib (H37), and Olaparib (H38) all yielded estimates near zero with confidence intervals crossing zero. This confirms that the negative prognostic impact of high NLR is a general baseline risk that does not specifically diminish the relative benefit of these targeted agents.\n\nIn summary, all proposed predictive interaction hypotheses are rejected. The inflammatory and clinical 'triad' appears to be purely prognostic rather than predictive of treatment response for the agents tested."}
